      "title": "Saved reverted diffs overwrite each other for attempts in the same run",
      "evidence": "destino = (self.run_log.parent / \"discarded\" / f\"{attempt.run_id or 'run'}.diff\")",
      "consequence": "When a run has more than one reverted attempt, each attempt writes its diff to the same file (keyed only by run_id), so a later attempt overwrites the earlier one, losing the earlier attempt's work — defeating the purpose of preservation. If run_id is empty, all runs share \"run.diff\".",
      "confidence": 0.9
    }
  ],
  "residual_risks": [
    "The method writes only the unified diff, not the full workspace state; if the diff generation itself is lossy (e.g., binary files), the preserved data may be incomplete.",
    "Writing is best-effort; a disk failure or permissions error silently returns \"\" and the work is lost, though this is by design."
  ],
  "untested_paths": [
    "The `_preserve_discarded` method is only called in the failure path with a guard and snapshot; no tests in the diff exercise it.",
    "The behavior when `attempt.run_id` is empty (fallback to \"run\") is untested.",
    "The interaction of multiple reverted attempts in a single run is untested."
  ]
}
